course: dictionary
lesson: 12935
title: Evident: easy to see
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Evident is an adjective. It means easy to see or understand, clear and obvious. If something is evident, anyone can notice it without searching. «Her joy was evident when she opened the present.» Everyone could see she was happy.
explain: In science and maths, evident often means shown by the results or the diagram. «It is evident from the table that the plant grew fastest in the sun.» You do not need to guess, because the numbers make it clear.
explain: A second, more formal meaning is shown by the facts, so people can be sure of it. Test it by swapping in clear or obvious. If the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning. «It is evident that the bridge is safe» means the facts prove it.
explain: Useful phrases include it is evident that, become evident, evident from, and self-evident. Evident and evidence share a root: evidence is what shows that something is true.
fact: Evident means easy to see or understand, clear and obvious.
fact: Evident can also mean shown by the facts, so people can be sure of it.
fact: «It is evident from the table that…» means the table shows it clearly.
fact: Synonyms for evident include clear, obvious and plain.
fact: «Self-evident» means so obvious that it needs no proof.
exercise: «Her joy was evident when she opened the present.» What does evident mean here? | *Easy to see | Sold out | Very quiet | Hidden under a blanket
exercise: «It is evident from the table that the plant grew fastest in the sun.» Where does the proof come from? | Guesses from the teacher | A story in a book | The weather forecast | *The results shown in the table
exercise: Which word means nearly the same as evident? | *Obvious | Hidden | Empty | Slow
test: «It is evident that the bridge is safe.» What does this tell you? | *The facts prove the bridge is safe | The bridge is very old | The bridge is made of wood | Nobody has checked the bridge
test: Which phrase means so obvious it needs no proof? | Well-known | Hard-working | *Self-evident | Half-hearted
exam: Which sentence uses evident correctly? | «I evident the answer quickly.» | *«The spilt milk was evident to everyone.» | «The evident pencil is in my bag.» | «The evident bus was late, so we walked.»
